From 50e94b658ee53c99e45080d8be4469940d904f4d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:55:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Non linear solver override

The option --non-linear-solver-override (-nlso), or else the environment
variable MFEM_MGIS_NON_LINEAR_SOLVER_OVERRIDE, read by
mfem_mgis::initialize, replaces the non linear solver of all the non linear
evolution problems (parameter NonLinearSolver, Newton by default) by the
one it describes, a name or the entries Name and Options in the syntax of
the data of TFEL, as the linear solver and preconditioner overrides.

The parameters given later by the program (setSolverParameters) are given
to the overriding solver, its own options taking precedence over the
parameters of the same name (its other options were applied when it was
built). If it refuses them (a parameter specific to another solver, such
as the StagnationRelativeTolerance option of the Newton solver), it only
receives the parameters accepted by all the non linear solvers and the
ones also given by its options; the other parameters are reported with
the reason of the refusal. The solvers read their options before applying
them, so a refused call leaves the solver unchanged. The override is
refused with --use-petsc, whose non linear solver replaces the ones of the
problems.

The name is checked by mfem_mgis::initialize against the names of the
factory (new method NonLinearSolverFactory::getNames), with the hints of
the optional libraries of the non linear solvers (KINSOL, SNES), now
shared with the factory. The module of the overrides is renamed
SolverOverrides. The replacements are reported once per message, so that
each replaced solver is reported when there are several problems; the
linear solver built by the program is no longer reported twice.

The refusals of the names of the preconditioner override are only tested
with the utilities of TFEL, which read the descriptions: without them, any
description is refused with another message.
